course: religion-ethics
lesson: 89
title: Owning up: the broken vase and the lost library book
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Owning up means saying "It was me" about something you did wrong, before someone else gets the blame or the problem grows.
explain: Ollie kicks a ball indoors and smashes Mum's vase; no one saw, and the cat is sitting right next to it, but blaming the cat is the oldest trick in the book: Adam blamed Eve and Eve blamed the serpent (Genesis 3:12-13).
explain: Owning up is scary for a minute and freeing afterwards; psychologists find children who own up are usually punished less and trusted more.
explain: Full owning up has no "but", and it opens the door to making amends: saying sorry, helping fix it, or paying for the damage.
explain: Christians believe God meets confession with mercy (1 John 1:9), and even King David, when the prophet Nathan said "You are the man!", answered at once, "I have sinned against the Lord" (2 Samuel 12:1-13).
fact: Nathan told King David a story about a rich man who took a poor man's only lamb; David was angry at the rich man, and Nathan said, "You are the man!", because David had done a worse wrong; a king could have punished him for saying it.
fact: "Whoever conceals their sins does not prosper, but the one who confesses and renounces them finds mercy" (Proverbs 28:13).
fact: Never let an innocent take the blame, not even the cat.
fact: The law of Moses made people pay for damage they caused (Exodus 21:33-34), and Zacchaeus paid back what he owed (Luke 19:8).
fact: Many countries' laws say a driver who hits a parked car must leave their name; honest cyclists do the same.
fact: Pretending you returned a library book blames the librarian, which is false witness (Exodus 20:16); a library is shared, so when one person lies about a book, everyone pays; the law said whoever lies about something left in their care must restore it and add a fifth (Leviticus 6:2-5).
fact: Staying silent while the whole class is punished for your fault is "false witness without words", as the vicar puts it.
fact: Adam and Eve hid "because I was afraid" (Genesis 3:8-10); fear of punishment and of looking bad drives lying.
fact: Confucius said, "To make a mistake and not correct it: that is the real mistake" (Analects 15.30).
fact: Owning up late is still worth it: the prodigal son came home after a long time, and his father ran to meet him (Luke 15:20).
fact: "A confession that excuses itself is half a confession," says the vicar; "I will confess my transgressions to the Lord" (Psalm 32:5).
fact: The law says to confess the wrong and make full restitution (pay back all of it), adding a fifth (Numbers 5:6-7); Jesus said to be reconciled with your brother before bringing your gift (Matthew 5:23-24).
fact: People forgive faster when you tell them than when they discover it themselves; "Confess your sins to each other" (James 5:16).
fact: The story of young George Washington, later the first President of the USA, chopping down his father's cherry tree and saying "I cannot tell a lie" first appeared in Mason Locke Weems's biography (1806 edition) and is almost certainly invented.
fact: Slang check: "eh" is a Canadian word added to the end of a sentence; "salty" means annoyed; "wisnae" (Scotland) means wasn't; Tim Tams are Australian chocolate biscuits.
exercise: Ollie smashed Mum's vase; the cat is next to it. What should he do? | Tell Mum, say sorry and offer to help fix or pay | Blame the cat, because cats can't be punished or told off | Hide the pieces under the sofa | Say the wind blew it off the shelf
exercise: Who was the first to blame someone else in the Bible? | Adam blamed Eve, and Eve blamed the serpent | Cain blamed his brother Abel when God asked where he was | Noah blamed the animals for the noise on the ark | Moses blamed Pharaoh when the people made the golden calf
exercise: Dylan's bike scraped a parked car. "Eh, nobody's out, just bike off," says his buddy. What is right? | Knock, or leave a note with a parent's number | Ride off quickly before anyone comes out of the houses | Scratch it a bit more so it looks old | Wait and see if the owner notices
exercise: Freya lost a library book and a letter comes. What should she do? | Tell the library; there are simple ways to replace or pay | Say she brought it back and the library lost it | Ignore the letter until they stop writing | Borrow a new book to make up for it
exercise: Why is saying "I brought it back" worse than losing the book? | It blames the librarian, which is false witness | Because libraries can fine people for lying | Because librarians always know who is lying | It's not worse; losing it was the real wrong, not the words after
exercise: Owen's ball broke the window. The class might lose break. What should he do? | Put his hand up and own up | Stay silent and hope someone else is blamed | Say the window was already cracked | Tell Miss it was a bird
exercise: Why is silence wrong when the whole class is punished for your fault? | It lets the innocent bear your fault, like false witness without words | Silence is never wrong in any situation | Because teachers can read minds | Because break is very important
exercise: "Was it you that ate all the Tim Tams?" Chloe, covered in crumbs, says "Nah... yeah." Why is owning up so hard? | Fear of punishment and of looking bad | Because Tim Tams make you forget what you've eaten | Because Chloe didn't really eat them, her brother did | Because mums never forgive anyone for eating biscuits
exercise: What did King David say when Nathan said "You are the man!"? | I have sinned against the Lord | Guards, take this prophet away | It was not me, it was my soldiers | A king can never be wrong
exercise: Leo says, "Bet David was well salty." What does "salty" mean? | Annoyed | Hungry | Thirsty | Happy
exercise: What did Confucius say is the real mistake? | Making a mistake and not correcting it | Making any mistake at all, however small it is | Correcting other people's mistakes in front of them | Admitting a mistake to a friend instead of a teacher
exercise: Josh hid a cracked phone screen for a week. Dad is angrier about the hiding. What should Josh do now? | Own up late, and say sorry for the hiding too | Keep hiding it, since it's far too late to say anything now | Blame his sister for the crack | Buy a case so Dad never sees it
exercise: Ewan says, "It was me that spilled it, but it wisnae ma fault." Is that full owning up? | No; a "but" cancels the apology; own it fully first | Yes; saying "it was me" is all that matters, the rest is detail | Yes, because the cup really was too near the edge | No, because Ewan should have blamed the cup
exercise: Hamid told Dad he broke the gnome. Dad says, "Let's say sorry together and offer to pay." What does this teach? | Owning up opens the door; making amends walks through it | Owning up is enough, no need to do more | Paying is enough, no need to say sorry | Gnomes are too cheap to bother about
exercise: Ruby deleted her brother's saved game. He hasn't noticed. What is best? | Tell him now; it's better coming from her | Wait to see if he ever notices | Say the game crashed by itself | Delete his other games so it looks like a bug
exercise: Is the cherry-tree story about George Washington true? | Probably not; it was almost certainly invented by Mason Weems | Yes; it is recorded in Washington's own diary from when he was a boy | Yes; it was seen by the whole village | No; it was really about Abraham Lincoln
test: Your brother says, "Just blame the cat, cats can't get grounded." What is the best reply? | Letting anyone else take the blame, even the cat, is the oldest trick and still wrong | Good idea, the cat won't mind | Blame the dog instead, he's bigger | Blame the window for being too close
test: "Eh, bud, just bike off, it's only a scratch," says Dylan's buddy in Toronto. What does the law expect of drivers in many countries? | To leave their name if they hit a parked car | To drive off as fast as they can so nobody sees them | To scratch the other side so the car looks even again | To wait in the car until the owner happens to find them
test: A cartoon shows a boy who breaks a window, says nothing, and the whole class cheers him at the end. What is wrong? | Staying silent let the class be punished for his fault | Nothing; that is exactly how it would really go in a real school | The class should have broken another window | He should have blamed the teacher
test: Chloe's mum says, "Thanks for owning up about the Tim Tams." Chloe says, "That was way easier than I thought." What does this show? | Owning up is scary for a minute and freeing afterwards | Mums never mind about biscuits, so there was nothing to be afraid of | Chloe should eat more Tim Tams | Owning up is only easy with biscuits
test: "Crap, I've had this cracked screen a week, Dad'll kill me," says Josh. What is the best advice? | Tell him now; late is better than never, and say sorry for hiding it | Wait another week or two until Dad's in a really good mood, then tell him | Sell the phone and say it was stolen | Blame the crack on the shop
test: Ewan's teacher says, "Sorry with a 'but' is half a sorry." What would be better? | I spilled it, I'm sorry, how can I help fix it? | I spilled it, but the cup was far too close to the edge | It wasn't me, it was the cup | Sorry, but laptops shouldn't be on tables
test: Why might David have punished Nathan instead of owning up? | He was king, and kings could punish anyone who accused them | Because Nathan told a lie about the lamb | Because Nathan stole the poor man's lamb | Because David didn't understand the story
test: What would happen if Ruby waits and her brother finds the deleted game himself? | He would likely be angrier and slower to forgive | He would forgive her faster, because she said nothing | He would never mind about it, since it's only a game | He would think the game had simply deleted itself
test: Freya's friend says, "Libraries are rich, they don't care about one book." What is the best answer? | Libraries are shared; when one person lies, everyone pays | She's right, so it's fine to lie about it to save a bit of money | Libraries make money from lost books | Only librarians pay for lost books
test: Ms Byrne says the cherry-tree story was probably invented. Your mate says, "So it's a lie and useless." What is fair? | A story can teach truth if we know it's a story; it's honest to say which is which | Yes, every made-up story is useless | No, it's definitely true, Washington said so | Stories should never be used to teach anything
test: Hamid asks, "Why give back more than the gnome cost?" What does Numbers 5:6-7 say? | Confess the wrong and make full restitution, adding a fifth | Pay back exactly half of the cost, and the priest pays the rest | Pay nothing if you said sorry first | Pay ten times as much as it cost
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the act of owning up? | The voluntary acknowledgement of one's own wrongdoing prior to another's being blamed | The attribution of one's wrongdoing to another party | The concealment of wrongdoing until its discovery by others | The acknowledgement of wrongdoing committed exclusively by others, and never by oneself
exam: Select the statement consistent with the account recorded in 2 Samuel 12:1-13. | Upon being confronted by Nathan, David immediately acknowledged that he had sinned against the Lord | Upon being confronted by Nathan, David ordered his imprisonment | Nathan acknowledged having stolen the poor man's lamb | David attributed the wrongdoing to his soldiers
exam: Given that a pupil remains silent whilst the entire class is penalised for that pupil's fault, identify the moral characterisation offered in this lesson. | A form of false witness committed without words | A commendable exercise of discretion and self-restraint | A matter of no moral significance, the teacher being responsible | A form of loyalty to the teacher and to the class
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the anecdote of George Washington and the cherry tree is consistent with historical scholarship. | The anecdote originated in the biography by Mason Locke Weems and is almost certainly fictitious | The anecdote is attested in Washington's own correspondence | The anecdote was first recorded by Washington's father | The anecdote is confirmed by archaeological evidence
